UID:fa0c8f8a670d6d6cfd5d8f9a7817@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Film + TV\,Atlanta\,United States
DESCRIPTION:4 LITtlE GIRLS is Spike Lee’s first feature-length documenta
 ry. The film asks audiences to relive the horrific tragedy of the Sixteent
 h Street Baptist Church bombing in Birmingham\, Alabama on a Sunday mornin
 g in 1963. It poignantly examines not only what happened but more importan
 tly\, the lives of the girls killed and the lives of those left behind. 
 “Only a filmmaker so resourceful\, so entirely confident in his practice
 d craft\, could have made such a quilt out of patches of nightmare and nos
 talgia – of gospels\, ghosts\, and grief. And only Lee\, having earned t
 he trust of these people\, could have persuaded them to talk to him” (NE
 W YORK MAGAZINE). Film scholar Delphine Letort writes about Lee’\nCatego
 ry: Film + TV\nOriginal listing: https://www.artsatl.org/event/the-emory-c
 inematheque-presents-4-little-girls-1997/
LOCATION:White Hall 208\, 301 Dowman Dr.\, Atlanta\, GA\, 30322
STATUS:CONFIRMED
URL:https://www.artsatl.org/event/the-emory-cinematheque-presents-4-little
 -girls-1997/
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-REGION:us
X-GLOBAL-CULTURE-SOURCE:artsatl
END:VEVENT
